# ▓▒░ WATCHDOGS ░▒▓
### ┤ Memory Controller & Security Logic ├

> **SYSTEM ALERT:** A hardware-level memory protection module intercepting all CPU-to-RAM traffic. Enforcing strict **base & limit register** bounds checking. Illegal access triggers a hardware-style exception (**TRAP**). The data never reaches RAM. 

`[ SYSTEM ]` Online
`[ MODULE ]` Member 2 of 3 // COA Project
`[ STATUS ]` :: SECURED

---

### ▓▒░ NETWORK MAP ░▒▓

```text
     memory request                     validated request
┌─────┐ ──────────► ┌──────────────────────┐ ──────────► ┌─────┐
│ CPU │             │  MEMORY CONTROLLER   │             │ RAM │
│ M1  │ ◄────────── │   (the Watchdog)     │ ◄────────── │ M3  │
└─────┘  ok / TRAP  └──────────────────────┘ read/write  └─────┘
```

`> SYS_MSG: CPU isolation enforced. Every read, write, and fetch goes through the watchdog.`

---

### ▓▒░ HARDWARE SIMULATION ░▒▓

| CONCEPT | IMPLEMENTATION |
|---|---|
| Base & limit registers | each `MemoryRegion` holds a `(base, limit)` pair |
| Memory bounds checking | `base ≤ addr` **AND** `addr + size ≤ base + limit` |
| Multi-byte / off-by-one | an access must fit *entirely* inside its region |
| Hardware exceptions | `MemoryViolationError` — simulated segfault |
| Permission bits (R/W/X) | `AccessType` flags, including **NX** (no-execute) |
| Address-space bounds | 16-bit space, `0x0000`–`0xFFFF` |
| Security overhead | per-check cycle cost → protected vs. unprotected benchmark |

#### > EXECUTION PROTOCOL (HARDWARE ORDER)
```text
[1] size sanity                 (size >= 1)
[2] inside the address space    (0x0000 <= addr < 0xFFFF)
[3] a region owns the address   (base <= addr < base + limit)
[4] the whole access fits       (addr + size <= base + limit)   ← OFF-BY-ONE KILLER
[5] permissions allow it        (R / W / X bits)
```
`> SYS_MSG: All five pass = request forwarded to RAM. Any failure = TRAP.`

---

### ▓▒░ THREAT MITIGATION ░▒▓

| ATTACK VECTOR | EXAMPLE | BLOCKED BECAUSE... |
|---|---|---|
| Buffer overflow | 100-byte write into 10-byte alloc | `addr + size` crosses the limit |
| Off-by-one overflow | 2-byte write starting at last valid byte | same check — naive checkers miss it |
| Unmapped memory | read @ `0x0500` | no region owns the address |
| Code injection | write into read-only code region | code region is locked `R-X` |
| Data execution (NX/DEP) | instruction fetch from stack | stack has no EXECUTE bit |

---

### ▓▒░ INTERFACE & API ░▒▓

| METHOD | ACTION |
|---|---|
| `map_region(name, base, limit, perms)` | Install a base/limit register pair |
| `unmap_region(name)` | Free a region — later accesses fault |
| `check_access(addr, size, access)` → `bool` | `True` = safe, `False` = attack |
| `read(addr, size)` | Validated read → forwarded to RAM |
| `write(addr, value, size)` | Validated write → forwarded to RAM |
| `fetch(addr, size)` | Instruction fetch path — requires EXECUTE |
| `dump_violation_report()` / `dump_stats()` | Report helpers |
| `reset_stats()` | Zero all counters between benchmark runs |

---

### ▓▒░ EXECUTION EXAMPLE ░▒▓

```python
from memory_controller import (MemoryController, SimpleRAM,
                               AccessType, MemoryViolationError)

mc = MemoryController(SimpleRAM())

# OS allocates a 10-byte buffer at 0x1000
mc.map_region("buf", base=0x1000, limit=10,
              permissions=AccessType.READ | AccessType.WRITE)

mc.write(0x1000, 42, size=4)                 # safe: 4 bytes fit in 10

try:
    mc.write(0x1000, b"A" * 100, size=100)   # attack: 100-byte overflow
except MemoryViolationError as e:
    print("Blocked by watchdog:", e)          # never reaches RAM
```

