### ▓▒░ INTEGRATION CONTRACT ░▒▓

**> MEMBER 1 (CPU Core & ISA):**
* Call `mc.read()` / `mc.write()` / `mc.fetch()` for every memory access. Never touch RAM directly.
* Wrap execution in `try / except MemoryViolationError` — that exception is your hardware trap. Abort the faulting instruction and jump to your trap handler.
* On `ALLOC`, call `mc.map_region(...)` so the watchdog learns the new boundary; on `FREE`, call `mc.unmap_region(...)`.

**> MEMBER 3 (RAM, Framework & Attacks):**
* RAM must expose `read(address, size)` and `write(address, value, size)`. Plug it in via `MemoryController(ram)` or `mc.attach(ram)`.
* For the with/without-security benchmark: run the same trace with `security_overhead_cycles=0` (unprotected baseline) vs `2` (protected), then compare `mc.stats["security_cycles"]`.

